Elections for the [[United States Senate]] will be held on November 8, 2016, with 34 of the 100 seats in the [[United States Senate|Senate]] being contested in regular elections whose winners will serve six-year terms in the [[United States Congress]] until  January 3, 2023. All [[Classes of United States Senators|class 3 Senators]] are up for election; class 3 was last up for election in [[United States Senate elections, 2010|2010]], when Republicans won a net gain of six seats. Currently, Democrats are expected to have 10 seats up for election, and Republicans are expected to have 24 seats up for election. However, as of June 7, only 9 Democratic held seats are in contention, as the Democrats have already secured California, with the top two finishers in the California Senate [[Nonpartisan blanket primary|jungle primary]] both being Democrats. Republicans, having taken control of the Senate in the [[United States Senate elections, 2014|2014 election]], currently hold the Senate majority with 54 seats.

The [[United States presidential election, 2016|2016 presidential election]], [[United States House of Representatives elections, 2016|2016 House elections]], [[United States gubernatorial elections, 2016|2016 gubernatorial elections]], and many [[United States elections, 2016|state and local elections]] will also be held on this date.

==Partisan composition==
All 34 [[Classes of United States Senators|Class 3 Senators]] are up for election in 2016; Class 3 currently consists of 10 Democrats and 24 Republicans. Of the Senators not up for election, 35 Senators are Democrats, 30 Senators are Republicans and one Senator is an independent who caucuses with the Senate Democrats. If vacancies occur in Class 1 or Class 2 Senate seats, the state might require a [[special election]] to take place during the [[114th Congress]], possibly concurrently with the other 2016 Senate elections.

